{"sender_id":"6b19db69-db29-4124-a317-7e98612efdde","event":"chat","schema_version":2,"history":[{"author_id":"6b19db69-db29-4124-a317-7e98612efdde","content":"I'm working on a gene expression analysis exercise in R. I already understand basic data frames, filtering, and p-values, but I'm still learning how to handle probe annotations.\n\nHere is my base R code:\n\n```r\ncolnames(annot)[1] <- \"probe\"\nmerged <- merge(results, annot, by = \"probe\", all.x = TRUE)\nannotated <- merged[!is.na(merged$symbol) & merged$symbol != \"\", ]\nsig <- annotated[annotated$adj.P.Val < 0.05, ]\nsig$direction <- ifelse(sig$logFC > 0, \"up\", \"down\")\nsig <- sig[order(sig$adj.P.Val), ]\nbest <- sig[!duplicated(sig$symbol), ]\nrownames(best) <- NULL\n\nnrow(merged); nrow(annotated); nrow(sig); nrow(best)\nbest\ntable(best$direction)\n```\n\nMy `results` table has 10 rows and 4 columns (probe, logFC, P.Value, adj.P.Val). My `annot` table has 9 rows and 2 columns (probe_id, symbol). The annotations include duplicate gene symbols, one empty symbol, and one probe without an annotation.\n\nPlease explain why each step is needed, not just what the functions do. Keep the explanation simple and short so I can understand and check it myself.","date":"2026-11-23T23:30:57.065Z"}],"date":"2026-11-23T23:30:57.065Z","message_id":"c8e593de-a9b3-40f2-8adc-cd5c7d2b27a2","thread_id":"d0ff339b-3064-4afd-b527-f66f01e031fe","editing":{},"acp_state":"running"}
{"sender_id":"6b19db69-db29-4124-a317-7e98612efdde","event":"chat","schema_version":2,"history":[{"author_id":"6b19db69-db29-4124-a317-7e98612efdde","content":"I'm working on a gene expression analysis exercise in R. I already understand basic data frames, filtering, and p-values, but I'm still learning how to handle probe annotations.\n\nHere is my base R code:\n\n```r\ncolnames(annot)[1] <- \"probe\"\nmerged <- merge(results, annot, by = \"probe\", all.x = TRUE)\nannotated <- merged[!is.na(merged$symbol) & merged$symbol != \"\", ]\nsig <- annotated[annotated$adj.P.Val < 0.05, ]\nsig$direction <- ifelse(sig$logFC > 0, \"up\", \"down\")\nsig <- sig[order(sig$adj.P.Val), ]\nbest <- sig[!duplicated(sig$symbol), ]\nrownames(best) <- NULL\n\nnrow(merged); nrow(annotated); nrow(sig); nrow(best)\nbest\ntable(best$direction)\n```\n\nMy `results` table has 10 rows and 4 columns (probe, logFC, P.Value, adj.P.Val). My `annot` table has 9 rows and 2 columns (probe_id, symbol). The annotations include duplicate gene symbols, one empty symbol, and one probe without an annotation.\n\nPlease explain why each step is needed, not just what the functions do. Keep the explanation simple and short so I can understand and check it myself.","date":"2026-11-24T04:26:31.476Z"}],"date":"2026-11-24T04:26:31.476Z","message_id":"9c7252b9-203b-4e46-98b2-0c80098b7e49","thread_id":"bdc2ae98-71ca-4cf1-b58d-6106d3d01304","editing":{},"acp_state":"running"}
